test_phello — Phase 1 chunk 10 printf-via-libc end-to-end
=========================================================

Drives the chunk-10 path: `pm_execve("/bin/phello", ["phello"])`
replaces the current process with a dynamically-linked C binary
that calls `printf("hello printf, sqrt(2.0)*1e6 = %d\n",
(int)(sqrt(2.0) * 1000000.0))`.

Compared to chunk-9's `test_dhello` (whose victim used `write(1,
...)` — a single direct syscall), `phello` exercises:

  * Multi-shared-lib install layout — libc.so now lives in
    `/usr/lib/libc.so` (FHS) instead of `/lib/libc.so`. ld-musl's
    default search path (/lib:/usr/local/lib:/usr/lib) finds it
    via SONAME after the PT_INTERP target loads at INTERP_BASE.
  * `printf` → vfprintf → malloc (FILE buffer) → __stdio_write
    → SYS_writev. Far more relocations than dhello's bare write.
  * `sqrt` resolves from libc.so (musl unifies math into libc).

The integer cast is deliberate. musl's `vfprintf` extends every
floating-point arg from `double` to `long double` via
`__extenddftf2`; on aarch64-linux-musl / x86_64-linux-musl that
soft-float helper isn't shipped by Homebrew clang and our
compiler_rt-stubs trap (`brk #0` / `ud2`) when invoked. Until real
compiler-rt builtins land, %f-style printf isn't viable. %d
sidesteps `fmt_fp` entirely.

A successful run prints `hello printf, sqrt(2.0)*1e6 = 1414213\n`;
any failure (file not found, ELF malformed, ld-musl fails to
locate libc.so under /usr/lib, etc.) falls through to this
program's tail and prints `phello: bad <step>`.

What this test would fail on if regressed:

  * Ramdisk doesn't ship `/usr/lib/libc.so` → ld-musl's needed-
    list lookup walks default search path and runs out, prints
    "Error loading shared library libc.so" before relocation.
  * `printf` reaching libc but stdio buffering broken → may
    fault inside `vfprintf`'s `malloc(BUFSIZ)` if the heap is
    misconfigured for the dynamic process.
  * `sqrt` symbol not resolved → relocation pass in ld-musl
    aborts before running main.

    module oskit.bin.test_phello

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with, pm_execve}


    print_idec(v: int)
        var n = v
        if n < 0
            puts("-")
            n = -n
        if n >= 10
            print_idec(n / 10)
        var b: [1]byte
        b[0] = byte('0' + (n % 10))
        puts(string(&b[0], 1))


    fail(code: int)
        puts("phello: bad ")
        print_idec(code)
        puts("\n")
        exit_with(code)


Build argv as a single-element array and call `pm_execve`. argv[0]
is the program name per SysV convention; ld-musl reads it for its
own diagnostics (LD_DEBUG) but `phello`'s `main()` doesn't
introspect argv.

    main(argv: []string)
        var args: [1]string
        args[0] = "phello"
        val rc = pm_execve("/bin/phello", &args[0], 1)

Reaching here means execve returned with a non-success code — the
binary was rejected before the point of no return.

        if rc < 0
            fail(1)
            return

Or execve nominally succeeded but the new program let control fall
through to this code instead of running its own _start, which would
indicate a frame-rewrite regression.

        fail(2)
